# arvo-tensor. Design

Fixed-shape, type-level-capacity tensor wrappers. Every container binds
a `Cap` const generic to a homogeneous value type; every slot is
materialised at compile time; no heap. `#![no_std]`, no alloc,
stack-only.

The crate now depends on
`arvo-storage` (for `USize` and the strategy markers used in trait
bounds), `arvo-bits-contracts` (for the `HasBitWidth` and related
trait surface used by bit-bearing tensor consumers), and
`arvo-numeric-contracts` (for the `Predicate` family applied in
filter / select operations). No dependency on `arvo-bitmask` or any
other L1 / L2 crate. Tensor is value-generic, not bit-generic.

## Position in the stack

arvo-tensor sits at L2 as a peer of arvo-bitmask. The division is by
element domain:

- **arvo-bitmask**, fixed-shape bit containers (Mask\<W\> chassis,
 BitMatrix\<W, N\> chassis over bits). Every slot is a bit, ops are
 bitwise.
- **arvo-tensor**, fixed-shape value containers (Array<T, N>, Matrix<W, N>
 over arbitrary T). Every slot is a T, ops are trait-generic over T.

Every algorithm crate (arvo-graph / arvo-sparse / arvo-comb / arvo-spectral)
depends on whichever of the two its input shape matches. When an algorithm
mixes both (e.g. graph adjacency as bitmask + node weights as tensor), it
depends on both.

## cap_size, canonical Cap → usize bridge

```rust
#[inline(always)]
pub const fn cap_size(c: Cap) -> usize { /* c.0.0 */ }
```

Nightly `generic_const_exprs` requires a raw `usize` in const-generic
array-length position (language grammar constraint). `cap_size(c)` is the
canonical projection from `arvo::Cap` to that `usize`. arvo-bitmask and
arvo-spectral previously shipped local duplicates; both now re-export from
arvo-tensor. Tracked under #121 for revisit when rustc const-eval over
newtypes stabilises.

## cap, canonical usize → Cap constructor

```rust
#[inline(always)]
pub const fn cap(n: usize) -> Cap { /* Cap(USize(n)) */ }
```

The inverse of `cap_size`. A literal `Cap(USize(n))` construction is rejected in
const-generic position ("struct/enum construction is not supported in generic
constants"); a named `const fn` is the accepted form, the same constraint that
forces `cap_size` to exist for the other direction. `cap` is the bridge a
consumer uses to form a `Cap`-parameterised type (such as `Csr<{cap(N)}, ..>`)
from a `usize` const-param. Same `#121` tracking as `cap_size`.

## Capacity and Dim, the GCE-free capacity foundation

Fixed-capacity storage where the capacity is a TYPE, not a `Cap` const
generic. A `Cap`-parameterised container stores `[T; cap_size(N)]`, which
places a const expression in type position and invokes nightly
`generic_const_exprs`. That holds when the capacity is concrete at the use
site, but the round-trip `cap_size(cap(N))` overflows the well-formedness
evaluator and ICEs the moment a generic consumer threads the capacity
through its own generic code. `Capacity` removes the const expression from
type position entirely.

```rust
pub trait Capacity {
    // backing storage for T at this capacity; [T; N] for Dim<N>.
    // the AsRef/AsMut bound gives slice access without a const-generic
    // Index HKT, and lets 2-D storage be the composition
    // R::Array<C::Array<T>> with no separate trait.
    type Array<T>: AsRef<[T]> + AsMut<[T]>;
    // the typed surface capacity; Dim<N> maps to cap(N).
    const CAP: Cap;
    // build the backing array with v in every slot.
    fn filled<T: Copy>(v: T) -> Self::Array<T>;
    // build the backing array by invoking f(i) per slot; the indexed
    // companion to filled (Matrix::diagonal and index-built algorithms use it).
    fn from_fn<T, F: FnMut(USize) -> T>(f: F) -> Self::Array<T>;
}

// one generic marker, any exact size. the implementing TYPE is generic
// over const N: usize; the trait stays non-generic, so consumers bind
// C: Capacity with no const param and keep the type-dispatch property.
pub struct Dim<const N: usize>;
impl<const N: usize> Capacity for Dim<N> {
    type Array<T> = [T; N];      // plain min-const-generics, no cap_size
    const CAP: Cap = cap(N);     // cap() only in value position
    fn filled<T: Copy>(v: T) -> [T; N] { [v; N] }
    fn from_fn<T, F: FnMut(USize) -> T>(mut f: F) -> [T; N] {
        core::array::from_fn(|i| f(USize(i)))
    }
}
```

`[T; N]` is plain min-const-generics: no `cap_size`, no
`generic_const_exprs`. `cap(N)` appears only in the associated-const value
position, never in type position. The one bare `usize` is `Dim`'s `const N`,
the irreducible array-length-grammar root (array lengths must be `usize` at
the language level). It is the single permitted bare primitive in the
capacity-as-type convention, the same root that already forces `cap` and
`cap_size` to exist.

This is the escape `arvo-strategy`'s `BitsContainerFor` already uses for bit
widths (dispatch a width to a container type rather than computing a
length), generalised from picking a scalar container to picking an array
type.

`Array`, `Matrix`, `Csr`, and `BitMatrix` now bind a `C: Capacity` type
parameter in place of a `Cap` const generic, storing `C::Array<T>` rather
than `[T; cap_size(N)]`. The migration removes every `cap_size` from type
position, so the algorithm crates no longer invoke `generic_const_exprs`
over capacity arithmetic.

## Array<T, C: Capacity>

Rank-1 tensor. `#[repr(transparent)]` over `C::Array<T>`.

```rust
#[repr(transparent)]
pub struct Array<T, C: Capacity> {
 data: C::Array<T>,
}

impl<T, C: Capacity> Array<T, C> {
 pub const fn new(data: C::Array<T>) -> Self;
 pub fn from_fn<F: FnMut(USize) -> T>(f: F) -> Self;
 pub fn get(&self, i: USize) -> &T;
 pub fn set(&mut self, i: USize, v: T);
 pub const fn len() -> USize;
}
```

`get` / `set` take `USize`. The `.0` unwrap used for internal array
indexing lives inside the method body, not at the call site. The backing
store is reached through the `AsRef<[T]>` / `AsMut<[T]>` bound on
`C::Array<T>`; `len` is `USize(cap_size(C::CAP))`.

`Array<T, C>` implements `IntoIterator for &Array<T, C>` yielding `&T`
(so `for t in &arr {... }` works with standard Rust idioms) and
`IntoIterator for &mut Array<T, C>` yielding `&mut T` for mutation.

## Matrix<W, C: Capacity>

Rank-2 square tensor. Promoted verbatim from `arvo-spectral/src/matrix.rs`
(retired the local home). `#[repr(transparent)]` over the composition
`C::Array<C::Array<W>>`.

```rust
#[repr(transparent)]
pub struct Matrix<W: Copy, C: Capacity> {
 data: C::Array<C::Array<W>>,
}

impl<W: Copy, C: Capacity> Matrix<W, C>
where
 C::Array<W>: Copy,
{
 pub const fn new(data: C::Array<C::Array<W>>) -> Self;
 pub fn from_fn<F: FnMut(USize, USize) -> W>(f: F) -> Self;
 pub fn get(&self, i: USize, j: USize) -> W;
 pub fn set(&mut self, i: USize, j: USize, v: W);
 pub fn diagonal(&self) -> Array<W, C>;
}
```

The square shape is two nested capacity projections; no separate rank-2
type is needed. The inner-row `C::Array<W>: Copy` bound lets the
row-of-rows be built and copied. arvo-spectral re-exports via
`pub use arvo_tensor::Matrix;` so existing import paths keep working.

## Enumerator, USize-typed enumeration

`core::iter::Iterator::enumerate()` yields `(usize, T)`, raw usize
leaks. arvo-tensor ships a parallel trait with a blanket impl:

```rust
pub trait Enumerator {
 type Item;
 fn enumerated(self) -> impl Iterator<Item = (USize, Self::Item)>;
}

impl<T: IntoIterator> Enumerator for T {
 type Item = T::Item;
 fn enumerated(self) -> impl Iterator<Item = (USize, Self::Item)> {
 self.into_iter()
.enumerate()
.map(|(i, item)| (USize(i), item))
 }
}
```

Consumers that want typed indices call `.enumerated()`:

```rust
for (i, t) in arr.enumerated() {
 // i: USize, t: &T
}
```

Consumers that want raw iteration use the standard IntoIterator impl:

```rust
for t in &arr {
 // t: &T
}
```

The trait is blanket: any `IntoIterator` gets `enumerated()` including
types outside arvo-tensor.

## What this crate does NOT contain

- Arithmetic operations on tensors (Add, Mul, Sqrt), consumer concern.
 arvo algorithm crates supply trait bounds (Add + TotalOrd + FromConstant
 + Copy) and compose with arvo-tensor types freely.
- Sparse shapes: arvo-sparse's domain.
- Bit-shaped tensors: arvo-bitmask's domain.
- ML-style broadcasting, reshaping, transpose chains, not aligned with
 arvo's role. Consumers that need those build them on top
 of Array + Matrix primitives.
- Heap-backed growable shapes: no. Sizes are const.

## Consumers

- **arvo-comb**, passes `&Array<T, N>` inputs, uses `Matrix<W, N>` /
 `Matrix<Bool, N>` / `Matrix<USize, N>` for internal DP tables.
- **arvo-graph** (future round), bit-matrix adjacency via arvo-bitmask;
 per-node value tables via arvo-tensor.
- **arvo-sparse** (future round), sparse-matrix value storage backed by
 `Array<W, N>` where the sparse-structure layer permits.
- **arvo-spectral** (future round), dense-matrix workloads already use
 Matrix; migration is the re-import change.

Downstream consumers (hilavitkutin, clause) will also use arvo-tensor
directly where they currently pass raw arrays through arvo algorithm
crates.


Round-by-round history is recorded in `mock/research/arvo-tensor-history.md` (Tier 2).
